
# Xbox XDBF Toolkit & In-Memory Serialization Engine

[![C++ Standard](https://img.shields.io/badge/Standard-ISO%20C%2B%2B17-00599C?logo=c%2B%2B&logoColor=white)](#)
[![Qt Runtime](https://img.shields.io/badge/UI%20Framework-Qt%205.15%2B%20QtWidgets-41CD52?logo=qt&logoColor=white)](#)
[![Toolchain Target](https://img.shields.io/badge/Toolchain-MSYS2%20UCRT64%20(GCC%2014%2B)-2F3238)](#)
[![Memory Safety](https://img.shields.io/badge/ABI-x86__64--w64--mingw32-informational)](#)
[![License: MIT](https://img.shields.io/badge/License-MIT-yellow.svg)](https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT)

A systems-level binary serialization framework, zero-overhead parser, and GUI inspector for the Xbox 360 Database File (`XDBF`) container format and Game Profile Data (`.gpd`) compound files. 

Engineered to modernize legacy structured file pipelines, eliminate strict-aliasing memory hazards under modern ISO C++17 specifications, and interface natively with 64-bit Universal C Runtime (UCRT) architectures.

---

## 1. Executive Architecture & Systems Overview

The Xbox 360 runtime persists unstructured player metadata, localized text tables, cryptographically tied achievements, and raw asset blobs within specialized compound binaries denominated as **XDBF Containers**. Structurally, an XDBF file acts as a contiguous, low-overhead database containing fixed-width header descriptors, sparse free-memory allocation indexes, and dynamically mapped entry tables.


```

+-------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|                         XDBF Compound Container                         |
+=========================================================================+
|  Container Header (Magic: 'XDBF', Version, Alloc Table & Entry Offsets) |
+-------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|  Free Memory Defragmentation Table (Sparse Span Allocation Headers)     |
+-------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|  Entry Descriptor Table (Compound Keys: [Namespace Type + 64-bit ID])   |
+-------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|  Contiguous Payload Heap (Mixed-Endian Structs, Compressed / Raw Blobs) |
|   ├── Achievement Descriptors (Scores, Flags, Timestamp Vectors)       |
|   ├── UTF-16-BE Localized String Payloads                               |
|   ├── Portable Network Graphics (PNG) Raster Icons                      |
|   └── Synchronization Queues (Strict Sync Ordering Tables)              |
+-------------------------------------------------------------------------+

```

---

## 2. Binary Layout & Formal Specification

An XDBF file operates over strict Big-Endian (PowerPC 11172 / Xenon architecture) multi-byte word representations. Little-Endian hosts (x86_64) interacting with these structures require cycle-efficient byte-swapping pipelines across all multi-byte primitive loads.


```

```
   0               8              16              24              32 (Bits)
   +---------------+---------------+---------------+---------------+

```

0x0000 | 'X'           | 'D'           | 'B'           | 'F'           |  Magic Identifier
+---------------+---------------+---------------+---------------+
0x0004 | Version (High / Major)        | Version (Low / Minor)         |
+---------------+---------------+---------------+---------------+
0x0008 | Entry Table Offset (BE)                       | Entries Count |
+---------------+---------------+---------------+---------------+
0x0010 | Free Memory Table Length                      | Free Spans    |
+---------------+---------------+---------------+---------------+

```

### Entry Descriptor Schema

Each payload record within the container is indexed by a 24-byte header entry:

$$\text{Key} = \langle \text{Type} \in \mathbb{U}_{16},\; \text{Identifier} \in \mathbb{U}_{64} \rangle$$

```cpp
#pragma pack(push, 1)
struct XdbfEntryDescriptor {
    uint16_t  namespaceType;   // 1 = Achievement, 2 = Image, 3 = Setting, etc.
    uint64_t  recordId;        // Unique 64-bit Big-Endian Key
    uint32_t  payloadOffset;   // Absolute byte offset relative to file origin
    uint32_t  payloadLength;   // Size of payload block in octets
    uint32_t  flags;           // Reserved sync & allocation flags
};
#pragma pack(pop)

```

---

## 3. Core Engine Mechanics & Computational Subsystems

### In-Place Endian Inversion Pipeline

Direct reinterpret cast operations across mixed-endian boundary representations invoke undefined behavior under modern compiler optimization levels (`-O2` / `-O3`) due to strict type-aliasing rules ($\text{ISO/IEC 9899:1999 §6.5/7}$). The engine implements memory-safe in-place swapping via standard vectorization hooks:

```cpp
template <typename T>
inline void SwapEndianInPlace(T &val) {
    static_assert(std::is_trivially_copyable<T>::value, "Non-POD type passed to SwapEndian");
    if constexpr (sizeof(T) == 2) {
        val = static_cast<T>(__builtin_bswap16(static_cast<uint16_t>(val)));
    } else if constexpr (sizeof(T) == 4) {
        val = static_cast<T>(__builtin_bswap32(static_cast<uint32_t>(val)));
    } else if constexpr (sizeof(T) == 8) {
        val = static_cast<T>(__builtin_bswap64(static_cast<uint64_t>(val)));
    }
}

```

### Dynamic Heap Allocation & Compaction (`fmalloc`)

When injecting new payloads or expanding variable-length Unicode string tables, the engine evaluates available free blocks registered in the Free Memory Allocation Table (`freeMemTable`).

* If contiguous space $\ge S_{\text{required}}$, the block is allocated and the remaining delta is sliced into a new free span entry.
* If no existing span accommodates the payload, dynamic expansion occurs at the EOF boundary, updating the Master Entry Table in an atomic linear step.

